        "thinking": "Using `platform: \"browser\"` is causing issues because many packages use Node.js built-ins like `node:crypto`, `node:fs`, `node:stream`, etc. The CF worker has `nodejs_compat` mode which allows these built-ins.\n\nI should use `platform: \"node\"` in esbuild (even though we're targeting Workers, because nodejs_compat provides these APIs). Or I can mark all the `node:*` built-ins as external AND use `platform: \"neutral\"`.\n\nActually, the right approach for a CF worker with `nodejs_compat` is:\n\n\n- Use `platform: \"neutral\"` since that won't assume a specific runtime\n- Mark cloudflare-specific modules as external\n- Let the nodejs_compat flag handle the Node.js API polyfills at runtime\n\nUsing `platform: \"node\"` would be wrong because esbuild would optimize for Node.js specifically, not for the Worker environment. I need to find the middle ground where esbuild doesn't make assumptions about the target platform, and let the CF runtime handle the compatibility layer. I need to figure out the right esbuild configuration for this Worker build. Using `platform: \"node\"` would handle node builtins automatically, but it might bundle things differently than I want, so I'm weighing whether to stick with `platform: \"neutral\"` and explicitly mark node and Cloudflare modules as external instead.",
